One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when interacting with the operating system, which can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.IOException;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    // This method is vulnerable to command injection if 'userInput' contains shell metacharacters
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // The runtime.exec() method can execute arbitrary commands if the string is unsanized
        String command = "ping -c 1 " + userInput;
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;

public class SecureCommandExample {
    // This method is secure because it uses a ProcessBuilder with a command array
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // By passing arguments as a List/Array, the OS treats them as literal arguments 
        // rather than shell commands, preventing command injection.
        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder("ping", "-c", "1", userInput);
        pb.start();
    }
}
```

